#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Duty Board v3.130.0 — Payroll 2: Statutory Deductions & Nigerian
Compliance (pass 2 of 8).

The track's TEN-chapter deep module (sibling of Accounts 5): the
custody thesis at the payslip, PAYE under the reformed regime, the
computation walk, pension, the smaller statutory layer, remittance
mechanics, annual obligations, and the compliance architecture.
Statutory figures are taught as consultant-altitude orientation with
the adviser boundary bright throughout. 35-bank.

Deploy: apply -> commit -> then on the server:
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_payroll_pro.seed_payroll_pro_track

Anchored, idempotent. Requires v3.129.0.
Run from ~/frappe-bench/apps/duty_board.
"""

import io
import json
import os
import sys

INIT = "duty_board/__init__.py"
DATA_PATH = "duty_board/academy_payroll_pro_data.json"
SEEDER_PATH = "duty_board/academy_seed_payroll_pro.py"
CHECK_ONLY = "--check" in sys.argv

L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The state rides every payslip", "12", "<p>The Accounts track's fifth module taught the custody thesis for the trading taxes; this module is its payroll sibling, and the thesis transfers whole with one sharpening: <b>the statutory deductions on a payslip are custody money — collected from the worker or levied on the employer, held in passing, owed onward on a calendar — and the pension share carries a weight even VAT never did: it is a person's retirement, held in the firm's hands for seven working days.</b></p><p><b>The statutory inventory.</b> The streams that ride Nigerian payroll, catalogued for the module's map: <b>PAYE</b> — the employee's income tax, withheld by the employer as agent, remitted to the STATE of the employee's residence (Chapters 2-3 — the module's largest resident, reshaped by the reformed tax law); the <b>pension contributions</b> — the Contributory Pension Scheme's two shares (the employee's, deducted; the employer's, added) into the employee's own Retirement Savings Account (Chapter 4 — the fiduciary stream); <b>NHF</b> — the National Housing Fund's employee contribution where in scope (Chapter 5); and the <b>employer levies</b> — NSITF's employee compensation contribution and ITF's training levy where the firm is in scope (Chapter 5 — the streams the worker never sees but the employer owes). Each stream has its base, its rate, its collector, its calendar, and its evidence — and the module walks each at consultant altitude.</p><p><b>The employee/employer split.</b> The structural distinction every conversation needs: EMPLOYEE deductions (PAYE, the pension employee share, NHF) reduce net — they are the worker's money, withheld in trust; EMPLOYER contributions (the pension employer share, NSITF, ITF) sit ABOVE gross — they are the firm's own cost, the third number of module 1's vocabulary made itemised. The conflation of the two is the trade's standing confusion (the owner who thinks the 8% pension is the firm's cost; the worker who thinks NSITF came from their pay), and the consultant's untangling of it — one table, two columns — is frequently the first trust-building deliverable of a payroll engagement.</p><p><b>The adviser boundary, restated for this terrain.</b> The grammar of two prior tracks, verbatim and load-bearing: the figures this module teaches are ORIENTATION — the shape of the regime, the conventional rates, the calendars as commonly administered — not advice; Nigerian tax law has just been rebuilt (the reform Acts effective from the 2026 tax year, whose payroll provisions Chapter 2 orients), states administer PAYE with local variation, scope thresholds move, and the ADVISER and the accountant own the firm's current reading, confirmed at the pre-season review (Chapter 8) and recorded on the policy page, dated. The consultant's identity sentence, payroll edition: <i>I build the machinery that computes what your professionals confirm — I do not set rates, read reforms, or sign returns.</i> With the boundary bright, the depth begins.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — PAYE under the reformed regime", "14", "<p>The module's largest stream, in its new landscape: PAYE after the Nigeria Tax Act 2025 — the reform that rebuilt personal income taxation from the 2026 tax year. The regime chapter, at consultant altitude.</p><p><b>The reform's shape.</b> What changed, oriented: the old regime's Consolidated Relief Allowance is GONE — replaced by a new architecture: an <b>exemption floor</b> (annual taxable incomes up to ₦800,000 attract zero tax — the reform's headline mercy for low earners, which for a trading firm's junior roles can mean whole grades paying no PAYE at all), a <b>rent relief</b> (a deduction of 20% of annual rent paid, capped at ₦500,000 — claimed on the employee's declared rent, which makes the rent DECLARATION a new payroll input the system must capture and file: Chapter 8's architecture), and a <b>new band structure</b> above the floor (progressive rates stepping up through the annual income bands the Act sets, reaching the top marginal rate at the highest incomes — the bands' exact edges are the configuration's numbers, confirmed from the Act's schedule by the adviser, never recited from memory: the consultant knows the SHAPE — a zero band, then rising steps — and configures the current edges). The allowable deductions survive the reform: the pension contribution, NHF where paid — each reducing taxable income BEFORE the bands apply (module 1's walk already used this; Chapter 3 formalises it).</p><p><b>State collection.</b> PAYE's administrative geography, unchanged in principle: the tax belongs to the STATE of the employee's residence — remitted to that state's revenue service (LIRS for Lagos residents, the respective SIRS elsewhere), which gives the multi-state firm a SPLIT remittance reality (the Ibadan branch's residents to Oyo's service, the Lagos staff to LIRS — the HR track's address data earning a tax consequence, and the employee's declared residence a governed field: Chapter 8's register carries it). The registration layer: the employer registered with each relevant state service, the employer's reference numbers on file, and the filing channels per state (the e-filing platforms where states run them) — the multi-state onboarding checklist the consultant builds once per client.</p><p><b>The transition posture.</b> The consultant's stance in a reform's early years, taught as craft: configurations carry the CURRENT law with the OLD regime's logic retired but documented (the parallel-run discipline at changeover — the reformed computation run beside the old for one cycle's comparison, differences explained per person before the first live month: the walk of module 1, at regime scale), the workforce COMMUNICATED (the reform that changes nets without explanation is module 4's query storm at its worst — the net that ROSE for junior staff under the exemption floor is good news worth announcing; the senior whose relief architecture changed deserves the walk), and the adviser's confirmation dated on the policy page — because 'the new law says' is the adviser's sentence, and the consultant's contribution is a system that changes regimes by changing formulas, not by changing spreadsheets (the reform-resilience argument module 1 sold, now cashed at the largest possible scale: the trade's spreadsheet payrolls are still mis-computing the old regime; the structured firm changed one configuration).</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The PAYE computation walk", "14", "<p>The mechanics, end to end — the annual creature computed monthly, walked once slowly so the consultant can narrate it forever. The computation chapter.</p><p><b>The annual frame.</b> PAYE is computed on ANNUAL taxable income against ANNUAL bands; the monthly deduction is an instalment of that yearly truth — so the computation ANNUALISES first: the monthly figures projected to the year (or the year-to-date actuals plus projection, per the configured method), the annual tax computed, and the month's share derived. The frame explains every mystery the spreadsheet era manufactured: why a mid-year increment changes the monthly tax by more than intuition suggests (the higher months re-project the year), why a bonus month spikes the deduction (module 5's variable pay entering the annual projection), and why December sometimes adjusts (the true-up — Chapter 7).</p><p><b>The walk, continuing module 1's case.</b> The ₦250,000-gross grade 2 employee, now at full statutory depth — annualised: <b>gross ₦3,000,000</b>. <b>Step 1, allowable deductions</b>: the pension employee share (8% of the ₦2.7m annual base = ₦216,000) — deducted from gross income before tax; NHF where the employee is in scope (2.5% of annual basic = ₦37,500 in this case, if applicable — assume applicable for the walk). <b>Step 2, rent relief</b>: the employee's declared annual rent, say ₦600,000 — relief at 20% = ₦120,000 (inside the ₦500,000 cap). <b>Step 3, taxable income</b>: ₦3,000,000 − ₦216,000 − ₦37,500 − ₦120,000 = <b>₦2,626,500</b>. <b>Step 4, the bands</b>: the first ₦800,000 at zero; the remainder walked up the Act's progressive steps — the configured schedule computing the annual tax (illustratively landing in the mid-₦200,000s annually for this income under the reformed bands — the precise figure is the configuration's output against the Act's schedule, which is the point: the consultant TRUSTS the configured bands they verified at Chapter 8's review, and never a remembered number). <b>Step 5, the instalment</b>: the annual tax ÷ 12 ≈ the monthly PAYE — the ~₦14,000-per-month figure module 1's walk previewed, now with its provenance shown.</p><p><b>The edges the walk must handle.</b> The cases every configuration meets in year one: the MID-YEAR JOINER (taxed on this employment's annualised income per the configured convention, with the prior-employment declaration where the practice requires it — the adviser's shape), the MID-YEAR INCREMENT (the projection updated from its effective date — the remaining instalments absorbing the change), the LOW EARNER (the junior whose annual taxable income sits under the ₦800,000 floor computes to ZERO PAYE — the configuration must produce a clean zero, not a small wrong number, and the payslip must show the zero honestly), the VARIABLE MONTH (module 5's bonus entering the projection in its month — the spike explained by the annual frame, and explainable to the worker BY the payroll seat because this chapter taught them the frame), and the MULTI-STATE person (taxed where resident, moved when residence moves — an effective-dated fact from the HR record, never a guess).</p><p><b>The narration discipline.</b> The chapter's closing craft note: the walk above is the payroll seat's core literacy — narratable on a whiteboard in four minutes — and the consultant certifies the seat exactly as module 1 prescribed: the person who can walk gross to PAYE by hand can answer every net-pay query, test every configuration, and survive every inspection conversation; the one who cannot is operating the academy's least forgivable black box, because this box computes people's take-home and the state's money in one pass.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Pension: the fiduciary stream", "14", "<p>The stream with the heaviest moral weight and the tightest calendar: the Contributory Pension Scheme — a person's retirement, computed on the firm's payroll, held in the firm's hands, owed to the person's own account within days. The pension chapter.</p><p><b>The scheme's shape.</b> The Contributory Pension Scheme (the Pension Reform Act's architecture, PenCom-regulated): every eligible employee holds a <b>Retirement Savings Account</b> (RSA) with a Pension Fund Administrator of THEIR OWN CHOICE — the PFA is the employee's decision, not the employer's (the HR record's PFA field, captured at onboarding per the joiner package, is a choice recorded, never assigned — and the employee's right to transfer their RSA between PFAs is theirs to exercise, with the record updated when they do). The contributions: the conventional minimum rates — <b>8% employee, 10% employer</b> — computed on the conventional base (monthly emoluments: basic + housing + transport — module 1's flagged base, doing here the exact work its flags were designed for), with voluntary additional contributions possible where the employee elects them (a governed deduction with its document, per module 5's machinery).</p><p><b>The remittance clock.</b> The scheme's defining discipline: contributions remitted to each employee's RSA (via the PFA's custodian) within <b>seven working days of paying the salary</b> — the tightest standing deadline in Nigerian payroll, and the one this module treats as sacred, because the unremitted pension is not late paperwork: it is a PERSON'S RETIREMENT sitting in the firm's account earning the firm's interest — the fiduciary breach that compounds (the missed month becomes the missed quarter becomes the multi-year hole discovered when an employee tries to access their RSA), attracts penalties with interest for the employee's lost returns, and — the reputational dimension the consultant states plainly — is the single statutory failure workers monitor personally (the RSA statement is the worker's own document; the firm whose remittances don't appear is caught not by an inspector but by its own staff, at scale, with receipts).</p><p><b>The mechanics.</b> The remittance's working parts: the contribution SCHEDULE per run (each employee: RSA PIN, PFA, the two shares, the base — generated from the payroll run's register, uploaded through the remittance channels the PFAs and custodians operate), the payment against the schedule (the separations of Accounts 3 governing the money movement as always), the RECEIPTS and upload confirmations filed to the evidence layer (Chapter 6), and the reconciliation habit: the per-employee remittance register (Chapter 8) against the schedules — because the pension stream's errors are PER-PERSON errors (the wrong RSA PIN sends a person's money into limbo; the stale PFA after a transfer bounces the upload), and the register that reconciles per person is the difference between a clean scheme and a drawer of unmatched receipts.</p><p><b>The employer's onboarding duties.</b> The checklist per new hire: the RSA confirmed or opened (the new worker without an RSA guided to choose a PFA and open one — the employer's facilitation duty), the PIN captured and VERIFIED (the digit-perfect field — module 1's masters-not-messages discipline applying to the number that routes a retirement), and the group insurance layer the Act couples to the scheme (the employer's life insurance obligation for employees — the policy the adviser confirms and the firm maintains, noted here because inspections ask for it beside the pension evidence). The chapter's one-line law, for the wall above the payroll desk: <b>the pension is not a deduction; it is a delivery — seven working days, every person, every month, evidenced.</b></p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The smaller statutory layer: NHF, NSITF & ITF", "13", "<p>The streams beyond the big two — smaller in naira, identical in discipline, and the ones most often discovered unpaid at diligence. The levies chapter.</p><p><b>NHF — the National Housing Fund.</b> The employee contribution to the federal housing scheme: <b>2.5% of monthly basic</b> (basic alone — a different base from the pension's trio, which is exactly why module 1's per-component flags exist), deducted from employees in scope (the scope rules — income thresholds and the voluntary dimensions the reform era has adjusted — are the adviser's confirmation; the consultant's posture is the configured flag per employee, set from the confirmed reading, reviewed at the pre-season), remitted to the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria on the monthly calendar, evidenced, and reflected on the employee's NHF record (the contribution is the employee's own — their path to the fund's mortgage products — which gives NHF the pension's personal character in miniature: the worker's money, trackable by the worker).</p><p><b>NSITF — the employee compensation stream.</b> The EMPLOYER'S levy (the worker never sees it): <b>1% of monthly payroll</b> to the Nigeria Social Insurance Trust Fund under the Employee Compensation Scheme — the insurance pool that compensates workplace injury, disability, and death (the scheme the Employee Compensation Act built), remitted monthly, evidenced by the ECS receipts, and CLAIMABLE against: the workplace injury the HR track's incident processes document is the event this levy insured, and the firm that pays NSITF but never files its certificate of compliance or knows the claims process has bought insurance it cannot use — the consultant's checklist adds the compliance certificate to the annual evidence file, because contracts and bids increasingly ask for it.</p><p><b>ITF — the training levy.</b> The employer's contribution to the Industrial Training Fund: <b>1% of annual payroll</b> for employers in scope (the scope thresholds — employee count and turnover tests — are the adviser's terrain and have moved with reform; the eligible small firm should confirm rather than assume either way), paid on the annual calendar, evidenced — and carrying the reimbursement dimension worth teaching: employers who TRAIN can claim partial refunds of the levy against approved training (the HR track's module 6 machinery — the curricula, the event records, the certificates — being exactly the evidence an ITF reimbursement claim runs on: the academy's training layer paying an unexpected statutory dividend, and the consultant who connects the two files has given the client money back).</p><p><b>The scope map & the levy calendar.</b> The chapter's consolidating artifacts: the SCOPE MAP (per stream, per the adviser's confirmed reading: who is in — which employees for NHF, whether the firm for ITF — dated, reviewed annually, because scope answered by assumption is the diligence finding waiting to happen), and the LEVY CALENDAR (every stream's remittance date on one page — PAYE's monthly state deadline, pension's seven working days, NHF's monthly, NSITF's monthly, ITF's annual — the compliance calendar of Accounts 5 gaining its payroll column, and Chapter 6 wiring it into the ritual). The smaller streams' collective lesson: none is large; all are findable; and the firm that runs them on the same ritual as the big two spends minutes monthly, while the firm that ignores them until diligence spends the difference in penalties, back-payments, and the deal-room's raised eyebrow.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Remittance mechanics: the custody ritual", "13", "<p>Accounts 5 built the six-step ritual for the trading taxes; this chapter adapts it to the payroll streams — same shape, tighter clocks, more collectors. The ritual chapter.</p><p><b>The six steps, payroll edition.</b> Per stream, per cycle: <b>1 — COMPUTE</b> from the run (the statutory register per stream generated by the payroll run itself — Chapter 8's architecture: the schedules are outputs, never separate spreadsheets, because the separately-maintained schedule is the version-drift disease at the custody layer); <b>2 — RESERVE</b> (the custody discipline of Accounts 5 verbatim: the statutory money moved to or marked in the custody position at the run, not discovered at the deadline — the reserve that makes the remittance a transfer instead of a scramble, and the conviction restated in payroll's sharper terms: the firm 'borrowing' the PAYE and pension for three weeks of cashflow is borrowing from its workers and the state at the most expensive interest in Nigeria — penalty rates plus fiduciary breach plus the trust of everyone whose payslip said the money was deducted); <b>3 — FILE</b> the returns and schedules per collector's channel (the state e-filings, the pension uploads); <b>4 — REMIT</b> against the filings (the payment separations governing); <b>5 — EVIDENCE</b> (the receipts, acknowledgments, and upload confirmations into the stream's dated folder — the evidence file that answers inspections in minutes); <b>6 — ZERO</b> (the custody account per stream returning to zero after remittance — Accounts 5's zeroing invariant, now multiplied across the payroll streams: the PAYE custody, the pension custody, each read post-remittance, each explainable or zero).</p><p><b>The custody accounts.</b> The ledger's payroll-statutory architecture (Accounts 2's payroll journal, now itemised): the run's journal crediting each statutory stream's liability account (the PAYE withheld, the pension shares, NHF, the levies accrued), the remittances debiting them to zero, and the month-end read (the accounts at their explainable levels — the post-remittance zero, or the current month's accrual awaiting its date, and NOTHING older: the aged statutory balance is the unremitted month wearing an account balance, and the Accounts track's trial-balance scan will catch it, which is the two tracks' machinery cross-checking exactly as designed).</p><p><b>The penalty economics, quantified in kind.</b> The owner conversation, equipped as Accounts 5 equipped it: each stream carries penalties for late or absent remittance (the percentages and interest per stream are the adviser's current numbers), and the structural point survives every rate change: penalties are computed on money the firm ALREADY COLLECTED — the fine is for failing to forward, which makes every naira of payroll-statutory penalty a pure loss purchased by disorganisation — plus the pension stream's interest-for-lost-returns dimension, plus the compliance certificates (the tax clearance, the pension clearance, the NSITF certificate) that bids and contracts demand and arrears block: the unremitted month costs money, and it also costs the next contract. The reserve discipline is the entire cure, and it costs a ledger entry.</p><p><b>The calendar, owned.</b> The chapter's closing wiring: every stream's dates on the levy calendar (Chapter 5), the calendar's lines OWNED (the named remitter, the named verifier — the two-names pattern at the custody desk), the seven-working-day pension clock tracked from each payment date (not month-end — the clock starts when salaries move), and the ritual's completion logged per stream per cycle. The firm running this chapter spends a predictable hour monthly; the firm that doesn't runs Chapter 10's patterns — and the consultant's install of this one page is, measured in penalties averted, frequently the highest-ROI hour of the whole engagement.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Annual obligations: the year-end layer", "13", "<p>The monthly ritual has an annual shadow: the returns, reconciliations, and true-ups that close each tax year. The January chapter — made boring by twelve clean months, exactly as Accounts 7 promised for the books.</p><p><b>The employer's annual return.</b> The headline obligation: the annual PAYE return to each relevant state — the employer's declaration of every employee, their annual emoluments, and the tax deducted and remitted (the return conventionally due in January for the preceding year — the deadline per state, confirmed current by the adviser), assembled not composed: the per-employee statutory register (Chapter 8) IS the return's content, and the firm that kept the register files by export while the firm that didn't reconstructs a year from payslips in the trade's least loved week. The return's counterpart obligations ride the same season: the projection declarations where states require them (the coming year's estimated payroll), and the reconciliations the state's system runs (the return against the year's monthly remittances — the mismatch that surfaces here is the unremitted or mis-referenced month, found by the collector if the firm's own reads didn't find it first).</p><p><b>The employee-facing layer.</b> The year-end's personal documents: the employee's evidence of tax deducted (the deduction certificates and payslip year-summaries the firm provides — the inputs to the employee's own Tax Clearance Certificate when they need one: the TCC that banks, embassies, and agencies request is issued by the state on the strength of the taxes the employer remitted in their name, which makes the firm's remittance discipline a PERSONAL service to every employee — the worker denied a TCC because their employer's remittances don't reconcile has been failed twice, and knows exactly by whom), and the rent-relief declarations refreshed for the new year (the reformed regime's annual input — Chapter 2's new field, re-declared and filed on the year's calendar).</p><p><b>The pension year-end.</b> The fiduciary stream's annual reads: the per-employee remittance reconciliation (the year's schedules against the register — every person, every month, matched; the unmatched upload chased with the PFA while the trail is warm), the RSA statement conversation (employees encouraged to READ their annual RSA statements against their payslips — the transparency that costs nothing and catches everything, because twenty-seven personal auditors beat one annual review), and the group life policy's renewal on the calendar (Chapter 4's coupled obligation, renewed before it lapses rather than discovered lapsed at a claim).</p><p><b>The true-up.</b> The annual frame's settlement (Chapter 3's projection meeting the year's actuals): the December computation reconciling each person's year — the actual annual income against the instalments deducted — with the final month absorbing the difference within the configured method's tolerances (the small December adjustment is the annual creature closing its books; the LARGE December swing is a configuration or data problem that the monthly walk should have caught earlier — the true-up's size is itself a health read on the whole year's computation). The chapter's closing economics, in the academy's oldest cadence: the annual season for a firm that ran Chapters 3-6 monthly is an export, a reconciliation, and a filing — days, not weeks; the season for the firm that didn't is the January scramble Chapter 10 convicts — and the difference was never January; it was the other eleven months.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — The compliance architecture: configured, registered, resilient", "13", "<p>The chapter where the module's law becomes configuration: the statutory layer wired into the system so that computation, registers, calendars, and reform-resilience are architecture, not effort. The machinery chapter.</p><p><b>The formulas, from the flags.</b> Module 1's design cashing at full depth: each statutory component a FORMULA on its flagged base (the pension shares on the flagged trio; NHF on basic; PAYE as the configured annual computation — the bands, the exemption floor, the reliefs, the allowable-deduction interactions all in the tax configuration the system computes per person per run), with the configuration's numbers sourced from the adviser-confirmed policy page and TESTED by the walk (Chapter 3's hand computation against the system's slip — per structure, at install and at every change: the go-live gate of module 1, now guarding the state's money too). The configuration's virtue restated as the sales argument it is: the reform that moves a band, a rate, or a relief is ONE configuration change, effective-dated, applied evenly — the structured firm absorbs tax reform in an afternoon; the spreadsheet firm mis-computes for months.</p><p><b>The registers.</b> The layer's memory, per stream, per person: the <b>statutory register</b> — each employee's monthly record per stream (the base, the computation, the deduction, the remittance reference) accumulating into the year — which IS the annual return (Chapter 7), IS the pension reconciliation, IS the inspection answer, and IS the employee's query answer (module 4's transparency reaching the statutory lines: the worker who asks 'where did my tax go' is shown their register row and the remittance receipt — the question answered by pointing, in the academy's oldest gesture). The register's inputs are the run's outputs by construction (no separately-maintained schedules — the version-drift fence), and its governed fields include the identity layer the streams route on: the Tax Identification Number, the RSA PIN, the declared residence state, the NHF number, the rent declaration — each captured at onboarding (the joiner package's statutory section), verified digit-perfect, and maintained as the effective-dated facts they are.</p><p><b>The calendar, wired.</b> The levy calendar (Chapters 5-6) living in the system: the remittance tasks generated per cycle with their owners and clocks (the pension's seven-working-day timer started by the salary payment date — the automation that watches the module's tightest deadline so no human has to remember it), the evidence uploads attached to the completed tasks (the ritual's step 5 leaving its trail where the inspection will look), and the completion log the compliance certificate season reads.</p><p><b>The pre-season review.</b> The annual architecture ritual, scheduled before each tax year: the adviser session (the year's rates, bands, thresholds, and scope readings confirmed — the reform landscape read by its owner), the configuration updated effective the year's start (versioned, dated — module 1's amendment discipline at the statutory layer), the component flags re-verified (the mis-flag hunt of module 1's review, now with the adviser in the room), the scope map refreshed (Chapter 5), the rent declarations collected for the new year (Chapter 2's input), and the walk re-run against the updated configuration (the gate, annually). One meeting, one afternoon of configuration, one tested walk — and the firm enters the year computing the current law evenly for everyone, which is the entire promise of this chapter's title: compliance as ARCHITECTURE — configured once, registered continuously, resilient by design.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Case study: HealthTrade's statutory year", "16", "<p>The statutory layer, installed on module 1's structures and run through a full year. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>The install & the regularisation (Ch. 1, 6).</b> The registration audit opens the engagement: PAYE registrations verified with the two relevant states (Lagos and Oyo — the multi-state map drawn from the HR records' residences), the pension employer codes confirmed, NSITF current — and ITF discovered UNREGISTERED despite the firm being in scope since crossing the threshold two years prior: the finding handled by the book — quantified, taken to the ADVISER, and regularised through the proper channel (the back-levy computed, the position negotiated by the professional who owns it, the registration completed) — the consultant's boundary held exactly as three tracks taught (<i>found and quantified by the machinery; resolved by the adviser</i>), and the diligence landmine defused before it could meet a deal room.</p><p><b>The configuration & the parallel run (Ch. 2-3, 8).</b> The reformed regime configured from the adviser session: the exemption floor, the bands from the Act's schedule, rent relief with its declaration flow (twenty-seven declarations collected in a week through self-service — the new input operationalised), pension and NHF formulas on their flagged bases. The parallel run compares the reformed computation against the old for one cycle: the junior grades' PAYE drops to ZERO under the ₦800,000 floor (nine people — the announcement at the branch meetings lands the reform as the good news it is, pre-empting the confusion), the senior computations walked and explained individually. The walk gates go-live: the ₦250k case computed by hand and by system — matching to the naira, first attempt this time (module 1's flag lesson having been learned).</p><p><b>The ritual, monthly (Ch. 4-6).</b> The six steps run per stream from month one: the schedules generated from the run, the reserve moved at computation (the MD's cashflow instinct meets the custody thesis in one sentence — <i>that money was never ours</i> — and the reserve discipline holds), the pension's seven-working-day clock beaten every month (the automation's timer plus the named remitter — the tightest deadline made routine), evidence filed per stream per month, custody accounts zeroing on the trial balance (the Accounts track's scan confirming the payroll track's discipline — the two certifications cross-checking in production). One catch proves the register: month five's pension upload bounces ONE person — a stale PFA after an employee's RSA transfer (the record updated within the week, the contribution re-routed, the person shown their register row and the corrected receipt: the per-person reconciliation catching the per-person error, exactly as Chapter 4 designed).</p><p><b>The annual season (Ch. 7).</b> January arrives boring: the annual returns to both states exported from the register and filed inside the deadline (the Oyo filing's first year taking an afternoon; the reconstruction it replaced had taken the accountant most of a prior February), the deduction summaries issued to all staff (two employees request TCC support that quarter — both clean, because the remittances reconcile), the pension year-reconciliation matching every person every month, the RSA-statement conversation held at the branch meetings (one employee's statement query traces to a PRE-system employer's gap, documented for their own pursuit — the register proving the firm's own year whole), rent declarations refreshed, the true-up landing small across the workforce (the annual frame's health read: the year computed clean). The MD's year-note closes the case: <i>the state rides every payslip — and for the first time, it rides in its own seat, on time, with receipts.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Common mistakes & the second law", "13", "<p>The statutory layer's scar tissue — the patterns behind most payroll penalties in the trade. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — statutory money as cashflow.</b> Symptom: the PAYE and pension 'borrowed' for stock; remittances timed to the bank balance; the deadline met in good months and missed in tight ones. Disease: custody treated as float (Ch. 1, 6) — borrowing from workers and the state at Nigeria's most expensive interest. Fix: the reserve at the run, the custody accounts zeroing, the calendar owned — the money that was never the firm's, never touched.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the unremitted pension.</b> Symptom: deductions on every payslip; nothing landing in RSAs; the multi-year hole discovered by an employee's statement. Disease: the fiduciary stream treated as the softest deadline when it is the hardest (Ch. 4). Fix: the seven-working-day clock automated from the payment date, the per-person register reconciled, the RSA-statement transparency recruited — twenty-seven personal auditors, invited.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the flat-rate guess.</b> Symptom: PAYE at a uniform percentage 'to keep it simple'; the exemption floor ignored; juniors over-taxed and seniors under-taxed, both wrongly. Disease: the computation replaced by folklore (Ch. 2-3) — and the reform era makes yesterday's folklore doubly wrong. Fix: the configured annual computation on the current bands, the walk as the gate, the parallel run at every regime change — and the workforce told, because the corrected nets are conversations either way.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the January scramble.</b> Symptom: the annual return reconstructed from payslips; the reconciliation mismatches surfacing at the state's system; February lost to archaeology. Disease: no register — the year never accumulated (Ch. 7-8). Fix: the statutory register as the run's output, the annual return as an export — January boring because the other eleven months were structured.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the scope-by-assumption levy.</b> Symptom: NHF deducted from everyone or no one by habit; ITF unregistered 'because we're small' past the threshold; NSITF paid but the certificate never collected. Disease: scope answered by assumption instead of the adviser's reading (Ch. 5). Fix: the scope map, adviser-confirmed and dated, reviewed annually — and the compliance certificates filed where the bids will ask for them.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the digit-drift identity.</b> Symptom: pension uploads bouncing; a person's contributions in limbo; tax remitted against a wrong or absent TIN; the residence state guessed. Disease: the identity fields ungoverned (Ch. 8) — the numbers that route money treated as free text. Fix: the statutory identity section of the joiner package, digit-perfect verification, effective-dated maintenance — masters-not-messages, applied to the fields that carry a retirement.</p><p><b>The second law.</b> Module 1 made pay a structure; this module seats the state in it: <b>statutory money is custody — the payslip's second passenger: computed from configured law on flagged bases, reserved at the run, remitted on each stream's clock with the pension's seven days held sacred, evidenced per stream, zeroed per cycle, registered per person, and reconciled until every worker's money reached every worker's account.</b> The firm that runs this law spends an hour a month and files January by export; the firm that doesn't funds the state's penalty book and its workers' distrust from the same account. Module 3 now assembles everything — the structures, the statutory layer, the HR packages — into the function's heartbeat: the payroll run itself.</p>"),
]

